All but the 
very oldest or very youngest of our contemporary readers belong to one of the 
following four generations: 
° “G.I.”’ elders, born 1901-1924, age 66 to 89 as 1991 begins; 
¢ ““SILENT’’ midlifers, born 1925-1942, age 48 to 65; 
‘“BOOMER’”’ rising adults, born 1943-1960, age 30 to 47; 
* “I13ER’’ youths, born 1961-1981, age 9 to 29.

But it is not our purpose to predict specific events; 
rather, our purpose is to explain how the underlying dynamic of generational 
change will determine which sorts of events are most likely.

